research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/cex-dirichlet-is-nowhere-continuous.md-1819-
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/cex-dirichlet-is-nowhere-continuous.md-1820-[L2] A point of $A$ is either a limit point of $A$ or an isolated point of $A$, and never both; $c$ is isolated in $A$ when $N_{\eta}(c) \cap A = \{c\}$ for some real $\eta > 0$ ([[def-limit-point-r]], [[def-neighbourhood-r]]).
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/cex-dirichlet-is-nowhere-continuous.md-1821-
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/cex-dirichlet-is-nowhere-continuous.md-1822-[L3] At a limit point $c$ of $A$, $f$ is continuous at $c$ if and only if the limit of $f$ at $c$ exists and equals $f(c)$; at an isolated point of $A$ every function is continuous ([[def-continuity-real]], [[def-function-limit]]).
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/cex-dirichlet-is-nowhere-continuous.md-1823-
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/cex-dirichlet-is-nowhere-continuous.md:1824:[L4] Heine criterion for limits: at a limit point $c$ of $A$, $\lim_{x \to c} f(x) = L$ holds if and only if $f(x_k) \to L$ for every sequence $(x_k)$ with $x_k \in A$, $x_k \ne c$ for every $k$, and $x_k \to c$. The direction from the limit to sequences is a theorem of ZF; the converse uses the axiom of countable choice exactly once ([[thm-sequential-criterion-for-function-limits]], [[def-countable-choice]]).
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/cex-dirichlet-is-nowhere-continuous.md-1825-
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/cex-dirichlet-is-nowhere-continuous.md-1826-[L5] Convergence of a real sequence: $x_k \to x$ when for every rational $\varepsilon > 0$ there is $K$ with $|x_k - x| < \varepsilon$ for all $k \ge K$; below every positive real lies a positive rational, so the test may equally be run at every real $\varepsilon > 0$ ([[def-real-limit]], [[def-sequence]], [[lem-rat-embeds-dense]]).
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/cex-dirichlet-is-nowhere-continuous.md-1827-
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/cex-dirichlet-is-nowhere-continuous.md-1828-[L6] Absolute value: $|u| \ge 0$, and $|u| = 0$ exactly when $u = 0$ ([[lem-of-abs-value]]).
