    40	---
    41	
    42	## Definition
    43	
    44	The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$, is the following
    45	statement.
    46	
    47	> For every family $(X_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by
    48	> $\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\mathbb{N}$ such that
    49	> $f(n) \in X_n$ for every $n \in \mathbb{N}$.
    50	
    51	Equivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most
    52	countable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.
    53	
    54	## Remarks
    55	
    56	- **The two formulations are equivalent, and the passage between them uses no
    57	  choice.** Given an at most countable family $\mathcal{F}$ of nonempty sets,
    58	  either $\mathcal{F} = \varnothing$, where the empty function is a choice
    59	  function, or a surjection $s : \mathbb{N} \to \mathcal{F}$ exists
    60	  ([[lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n]]); applying the indexed form to
    61	  $X_n := s(n)$ gives $f$ with $f(n) \in s(n)$, and
    62	  $g(S) := f(\min\{\, n : s(n) = S \,\})$ is a choice function for $\mathcal{F}$,
    63	  the minimum being canonical by [[thm-well-ordering-principle]]. Conversely a
    64	  choice function $g$ on the at most countable family $\{\, X_n : n \in \mathbb{N} \,\}$
    65	  gives $f(n) := g(X_n)$.
    66	
    67	- **$\mathrm{AC}_\omega$ is strictly weaker than the Axiom of Choice**
    68	  ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]): AC implies it immediately, since AC applies to every
    69	  family, while it is consistent with ZF that $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$ holds and AC
    70	  fails. **It is also strictly stronger than what ZF proves**: it is consistent
    71	  with ZF that $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$ fails, as Cohen's first model shows, since an
    72	  infinite set of reals with no countably infinite subset
    73	  ([[rem-cohen-first-model]]) is already a failure of $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$; the
    74	  Feferman-Levy model ([[rem-feferman-levy-model]]) is a second witness. Both
    75	  statements are conditional on the consistency of ZF and are external results,
    76	  established by forcing and by permutation models; they are recorded here with
    77	  references and are **not** proved in this library, which contains neither
    78	  technique. Of the two, only the failure of $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$ is recorded in
    79	  this library's catalogue of unproved results; the separation of
    80	  $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$ from AC in the other direction is quoted from the
    81	  references alone.
    82	
    83	- **Dependent choice sits between them.** The Axiom of Dependent Choice
    84	  (DC) says that if $R$ is a relation on a nonempty set $X$ such that every
    85	  $x \in X$ has some $y$ with $x \mathbin{R} y$, then there is a sequence
    86	  $(x_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ with $x_n \mathbin{R} x_{n+1}$ for all $n$. In ZF,
    87	  $\mathrm{AC} \Rightarrow \mathrm{DC} \Rightarrow \mathrm{AC}_\omega$; both
    88	  implications are theorems of ZF, and neither is proved here. That neither
    89	  reverses is a pair of relative-consistency results of the same kind as in the
    90	  previous bullet: if ZF is consistent, then so are ZF + DC + (not AC) and
    91	  ZF + $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$ + (not DC). Both are established by forcing and by
    92	  permutation models, are quoted here from the references rather than proved, and
    93	  cannot be stated without the consistency hypothesis; so "DC is *strictly*
    94	  between AC and $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$" is shorthand for those two conditional
    95	  statements and is never used here as a standalone assertion. DC is the
    96	  principle that legitimises "choose $x_0$, then choose $x_1$ depending on
    97	  $x_0$, and so on"; $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$ only legitimises countably many
    98	  *independent* choices made at once.
    99	
   100	- **Being an axiom, $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$ carries no well-definedness obligation**,
   101	  which is why this item has no `justified_by`. Its role in this library is
   102	  bookkeeping: [[thm-countable-union-of-countable]] assumes it and flags the exact
   103	  step that spends it, and [[fs-countable-union-theorem-of-zf]] records that the
   104	  assumption cannot be removed.
   105	
   106	- Every result *proved* on this page other than
   107	  [[thm-countable-union-of-countable]] is a theorem of ZF alone. In particular
   108	  [[lem-subset-of-countable]], [[lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n]],
   109	  [[thm-schroder-bernstein]], [[thm-rationals-countable]], [[thm-cantor-powerset]]
   110	  and [[thm-r-uncountable]] are choice free, and each says so. The false
   111	  statements at the end of the page are not all of that kind, and the claim above
   112	  does not cover them: two of the three refute a ZF-provability claim only under
   113	  the hypothesis that ZF is consistent, quoting an external independence result
   114	  rather than proving it, and they say so in their own Facts.
