    36	---
    37	
    38	## Definition
    39	
    40	Let $A \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ and $x \in \mathbb{R}$, with neighbourhoods as in
    41	[[def-neighbourhood-r]] and closure as in
    42	[[def-interior-closure-boundary-r]].
    43	
    44	- $x$ is an **adherent point** of $A$ when $N_\varepsilon(x) \cap A \ne
    45	  \varnothing$ for every real $\varepsilon > 0$.
    46	- $x$ is a **limit point** (or *accumulation point*) of $A$ when
    47	  $N^{*}_\varepsilon(x) \cap A \ne \varnothing$ for every real
    48	  $\varepsilon > 0$: every punctured neighbourhood of $x$ meets $A$.
    49	- $x$ is an **isolated point** of $A$ when $x \in A$ and there is a real
    50	  $\varepsilon > 0$ with $N_\varepsilon(x) \cap A = \{x\}$.
    51	- The **derived set** of $A$ is
    52	  $$A' \;:=\; \{\, x \in \mathbb{R} : x \text{ is a limit point of } A \,\}.$$
    53	- $A$ is **dense in $\mathbb{R}$** when $\overline{A} = \mathbb{R}$.
    54	
    55	**A limit point is an adherent point**, since $N^{*}_\varepsilon(x) \subseteq
    56	N_\varepsilon(x)$; and an element of $A$ is an adherent point of $A$, since
    57	$x \in N_\varepsilon(x) \cap A$ ([[def-neighbourhood-r]]). So the adherent
    58	points of $A$ are exactly the points of $A \cup A'$, a statement proved as part
    59	of [[thm-closure-characterisations-r]].
    60	
    61	**Limit point and isolated point are exact opposites inside $A$.** For
    62	$x \in A$: $x$ is an isolated point of $A$ exactly when it is not a limit point
    63	of $A$. Indeed $N_\varepsilon(x) \cap A = \{x\}$ says precisely that
    64	$N^{*}_\varepsilon(x) \cap A = \varnothing$, because $x$ itself always lies in
    65	$N_\varepsilon(x) \cap A$ when $x \in A$; so the existence of an $\varepsilon$
    66	witnessing isolation is the negation of the condition defining a limit point.
    67	A point of $A$ is therefore either isolated in $A$ or a limit point of $A$, and
    68	never both.
    69	
    70	**A limit point need not belong to the set, and a point of the set need not be a
    71	limit point.** Both possibilities occur, and the two examples that matter later
    72	are $0$, which is a limit point of $\{\, 1/k : k \ge 1 \,\}$ without belonging
    73	to it, and $0$ again, which belongs to $\{0\} \cup [1,2]$ as an isolated point.
    74	
    75	## Remarks
    76	
    77	- **Terminology: *limit point* here is about a set, never about a sequence.**
    78	  This library reserves *subsequential limit* for the sequential notion
    79	  ([[def-subsequential-limit]]), and the two are genuinely different: the
    80	  constant sequence $x_k = 0$ has $0$ as a subsequential limit, while its set of
    81	  values $\{0\}$ has no limit point at all. The distinction is the one
    82	  [[def-subsequential-limit]] records under "Terminology", and it is respected
    83	  throughout this page.
    84	
    85	- **Density is defined through the closure, not through intervals.** Saying
    86	  $\overline{A} = \mathbb{R}$ is equivalent to saying that every nonempty open
    87	  subset of $\mathbb{R}$ meets $A$, and also to saying that every neighbourhood
    88	  of every real meets $A$; the equivalences follow from
    89	  [[thm-closure-characterisations-r]] and are used in that form in
    90	  [[lem-q-and-irrationals-dense-r]].
    91	
    92	- **The derived set need not be comparable with the set.** It can be strictly
    93	  larger, as for $\mathbb{Q}$: every punctured neighbourhood of any real
    94	  contains a rational, since density supplies one strictly between $x$ and
    95	  $x + \varepsilon$ ([[lem-q-and-irrationals-dense-r]]), so the derived set of
    96	  $\mathbb{Q}$ is all of $\mathbb{R}$. It can be strictly smaller, as for
    97	  $\{0\}$, whose derived set is empty; and it can be neither, as for
    98	  $\{0\} \cup (1,2)$, whose derived set is $[1,2]$, a set containing points
    99	  outside the original and omitting the point $0$ of it. A closed set satisfying
   100	  $A \subseteq A'$ is called perfect ([[def-perfect-set-r]]).
