    33	---
    34	
    35	## Definition
    36	
    37	Throughout, $\mathbb{R}$ is the complete ordered field
    38	([[def-complete-ordered-field]], [[def-ordered-field]]) with its order
    39	([[def-real-order]]) and its absolute value ([[def-abs-value]]).
    40	
    41	Let $x \in \mathbb{R}$ and let $\varepsilon \in \mathbb{R}$ with
    42	$\varepsilon > 0$. The **$\varepsilon$-neighbourhood of $x$** is
    43	
    44	$$N_\varepsilon(x) \;:=\; \{\, y \in \mathbb{R} : |y - x| < \varepsilon \,\},$$
    45	
    46	and the **punctured $\varepsilon$-neighbourhood of $x$** is
    47	
    48	$$N^{*}_\varepsilon(x) \;:=\; N_\varepsilon(x) \setminus \{x\} \;=\; \{\, y \in \mathbb{R} : 0 < |y - x| < \varepsilon \,\}.$$
    49	
    50	The two descriptions of $N^{*}_\varepsilon(x)$ agree because $|y - x| = 0$ holds
    51	exactly when $y = x$ ([[lem-of-abs-value]]).
    52	
    53	**A neighbourhood is an open interval.** For every $x$ and every
    54	$\varepsilon > 0$,
    55	
    56	$$N_\varepsilon(x) \;=\; (x - \varepsilon,\ x + \varepsilon),$$
    57	
    58	the interval of [[def-interval]]. Indeed [[lem-of-abs-value]] gives, for
    59	$\varepsilon > 0$, the equivalence $|y - x| < \varepsilon \iff -\varepsilon <
    60	y - x < \varepsilon$, and adding $x$ throughout turns the right-hand side into
    61	$x - \varepsilon < y < x + \varepsilon$ ([[def-ordered-field]]).
    62	
    63	**The centre lies in its own neighbourhoods.** $x \in N_\varepsilon(x)$, since
    64	$|x - x| = |0| = 0 < \varepsilon$ ([[lem-of-abs-value]]).
    65	
    66	**Punctured neighbourhoods are never empty.** The element
    67	$y := x + \varepsilon/2$ satisfies $|y - x| = \varepsilon/2$, which is
    68	$> 0$ and $< \varepsilon$, so $y \in N^{*}_\varepsilon(x)$
    69	([[lem-of-abs-value]], [[def-ordered-field]]).
    70	
    71	**Monotonicity in the radius.** If $0 < \delta \le \varepsilon$ then
    72	$N_\delta(x) \subseteq N_\varepsilon(x)$, because $|y - x| < \delta \le
    73	\varepsilon$ ([[def-ordered-field]]).
    74	
    75	**Nesting at an interior point.** If $y \in N_\varepsilon(x)$ and
    76	$0 < \delta \le \varepsilon - |y - x|$, then
    77	
    78	$$N_\delta(y) \;\subseteq\; N_\varepsilon(x).$$
    79	
    80	Indeed for $z \in N_\delta(y)$ the triangle inequality
    81	([[lem-of-triangle-inequality]]) gives
    82	$|z - x| = |(z - y) + (y - x)| \le |z - y| + |y - x| < \delta + |y - x| \le
    83	\varepsilon$. Note that $\varepsilon - |y - x| > 0$ precisely because
    84	$y \in N_\varepsilon(x)$, so such a $\delta$ always exists.
    85	
    86	## Remarks
    87	
    88	- **The radius is a real number, not a rational.** Nothing on this page tests a
    89	  condition against rational radii only. That convention belongs to
    90	  [[def-real-limit]], where the quantifier is over rational $\varepsilon$ and
    91	  the passage between the rational and the real form is the sanctioned remark of
    92	  [[def-sequence]]. Here $\varepsilon$ ranges over the positive reals
    93	  throughout, and every statement above is proved for an arbitrary positive
    94	  real.
    95	
    96	- **Why the punctured version is separated out.** A limit point of a set is a
    97	  point every punctured neighbourhood of which meets the set
    98	  ([[def-limit-point-r]]), and deleting the centre is exactly what stops a point
    99	  of the set from qualifying automatically. The unpunctured condition defines
   100	  the weaker notion of an adherent point, and the difference between the two is
   101	  precisely an isolated point.
   102	
   103	- **Nesting is the workhorse.** Almost every openness verification on this page
   104	  has the shape "given $y$ in the set, shrink the radius by the distance
   105	  already travelled", which is the nesting property above. It is recorded here
   106	  once so that no later proof has to redo the triangle inequality in passing.
