     2	page: continuity-ivt-evt-and-uniform-continuity
     3	title: "Continuity, IVT, EVT, and Uniform Continuity"
     4	status: published
     5	items: [def-continuity-real, thm-sequential-criterion-for-continuity,
     6	        thm-algebra-of-continuous-functions,
     7	        thm-composition-of-continuous-functions,
     8	        thm-continuity-preimage-characterisation,
     9	        thm-continuous-image-of-a-compact-set-r, cor-boundedness-theorem-r,
    10	        thm-extreme-value-r, thm-intermediate-value,
    11	        cor-continuous-image-of-an-interval-is-an-interval,
    12	        thm-one-dimensional-fixed-point,
    13	        lem-monotone-with-interval-image-is-continuous,
    14	        def-uniform-continuity-real, lem-real-and-metric-notions-agree,
    15	        thm-heine-cantor-r, cor-continuous-extension-from-a-dense-subset-r,
    16	        thm-holder-exponent-above-one-forces-constancy,
    17	        thm-compactness-is-necessary-for-evt-and-uniform-continuity]
    18	examples: [fs-continuity-implies-uniform-continuity,
    19	           fs-continuous-image-of-a-closed-set-is-closed,
    20	           fs-evt-holds-on-every-bounded-domain]
    21	---
    22	
    23	**Objective.** This page defines continuity of a real function on a subset of
    24	$\mathbb{R}$, proves the toolkit that makes the notion usable, and then proves
    25	the four theorems that continuity exists for: the image of a compact set is
    26	compact, the extreme value theorem, the intermediate value theorem, and
    27	Heine-Cantor. Along the way it states the dictionary that
    28	reconciles the vocabulary of this page with the metric-space vocabulary of the
    29	earlier topology track, and it closes with counterexamples showing that
    30	compactness is needed for the extreme-value and uniform-continuity conclusions;
    31	the intermediate value theorem instead rests on the interval/connectedness
    32	hypothesis.
    33	
    34	**The definition, and the clause that is easy to get wrong.**
    35	[[def-continuity-real]] says that $f : A \to \mathbb{R}$ is continuous at
    36	$c \in A$ when for every real $\varepsilon > 0$ there is a real $\delta > 0$
    37	with $|f(x) - f(c)| < \varepsilon$ for every $x \in A$ satisfying
    38	$|x - c| < \delta$. Note the two differences from the limit of the previous
    39	page: the point $c$ is required to lie in $A$, and the condition is
    40	**unpunctured**, so $x = c$ is allowed. Three clauses follow, and all three are
    41	part of the definition. At a limit point of $A$, continuity at $c$ is exactly
    42	$\lim_{x \to c} f(x) = f(c)$; at an **isolated** point of $A$ every function is
    43	continuous, because the only admissible $x$ is $c$ itself; and continuity on $A$
    44	is continuity at each point of $A$. The second clause is not the vacuous formula
    45	that [[fs-limit-unique-at-every-point-of-the-domain]] warns about: there the
    46	punctured formula was satisfied by every real $L$ at once and so defined
    47	nothing, whereas here the condition names one well-defined property, and the
    48	symbol $\lim_{x \to c} f(x)$ simply does not exist at an isolated point.
    49	
    50	**Choice hygiene, and why it shapes the page.**
    51	[[thm-sequential-criterion-for-continuity]] proves that $f$ is continuous at $c$
    52	if and only if $f(x_k) \to f(c)$ for every sequence in $A$ converging to $c$ —
    53	sequences that may hit $c$, which is the difference from
    54	[[thm-sequential-criterion-for-function-limits]] and what makes the criterion
    55	meaningful at an isolated point. The direction from continuity to sequences is a
    56	theorem of ZF; the converse inherits the single use of countable choice made on
    57	the previous page, and [[rem-heine-criterion-choice-cost]] records exactly what
    58	is and is not claimed about that. As on that page, everything provable from
    59	$\varepsilon$ and $\delta$ is proved that way: the algebra of continuous
    60	functions, composition, the preimage characterisation and the intermediate value
    61	theorem spend no choice at all. The two items that do spend it,
    62	[[thm-heine-cantor-r]] and the converse direction of the sequential criterion,
    63	say so in their own statements.
    64	
    65	**The toolkit.** [[thm-algebra-of-continuous-functions]] proves that sums,
    66	scalar multiples, products, absolute values, maxima, minima and quotients with
    67	nonvanishing denominator of continuous functions are continuous, and adds the
    68	clause the rest of the page uses constantly: constants, the identity, every
    69	power $x \mapsto x^{n}$ and every polynomial function are continuous on every
    70	subset of $\mathbb{R}$. [[thm-composition-of-continuous-functions]] proves that
    71	a composite of continuous functions is continuous with **no** side hypothesis —
    72	in contrast with [[thm-composition-of-function-limits]], which needs one of two
    73	extra conditions and is false without both
    74	([[fs-naive-composition-of-limits]]). The reason is visible in one line: the
    75	unpunctured quantifier of [[def-continuity-real]] controls $f$ at the very value
    76	the limit version cannot see. [[thm-continuity-preimage-characterisation]] then
    77	gives the topological form: $f$ is continuous on $A$ exactly when the preimage
    78	of every open set is the trace on $A$ of an open subset of $\mathbb{R}$, and
    79	dually for closed sets. The preimage lives in $A$, not in $\mathbb{R}$, so this
    80	does not say that preimages of open sets are open; and the witnessing open set
    81	is built as a single union over a family cut out by a property, so no choice
    82	function on an arbitrary subset of $\mathbb{R}$ is needed.
    83	
    84	**Compactness, and the two theorems it buys.**
    85	[[thm-continuous-image-of-a-compact-set-r]] proves that the image of a compact
    86	subset of $\mathbb{R}$ under a continuous function is compact, by an
    87	$\mathbb{R}$-native cover argument in which the only selection is from a finite
    88	list. [[cor-boundedness-theorem-r]] reads off boundedness, and
    89	[[thm-extreme-value-r]] reads off attainment: on a nonempty compact $K$ a
    90	continuous $f$ satisfies $\sup f[K] = f(p)$ and $\inf f[K] = f(q)$ for some
    91	$p, q \in K$. The two ingredients are kept apart in that proof — compactness
    92	enters only through compactness of the image, order-completeness only through
    93	the existence of $\sup$ and $\inf$, and closedness of the image is the bridge.
    94	
    95	**The intermediate value theorem, by a canonical bisection.**
    96	[[thm-intermediate-value]] proves that a continuous function on $[a,b]$ takes
    97	every value between $f(a)$ and $f(b)$. The bisection tests the left half first
    98	and takes the right half only when the left is excluded, so the passage from one
    99	interval to the next is a *function* of that interval and
   100	[[thm-recursion]] applies with nothing selected;
   101	[[thm-nested-interval-property]] then supplies the point, and only the
   102	choice-free direction of the sequential criterion is used to pass to the limit.
   103	[[cor-continuous-image-of-an-interval-is-an-interval]] draws the two standard
   104	consequences: the image of an order-convex set is order-convex, hence connected
   105	([[thm-connected-subsets-of-r-are-intervals]]), and the image of a closed
   106	bounded interval is a closed bounded interval. [[thm-one-dimensional-fixed-point]]
   107	is the third: a continuous map of $[a,b]$ into itself has a fixed point, because
   108	$\varphi(x) - x$ changes sign. **The existence of $k$-th roots is not restated
   109	here**: [[thm-nth-roots-exist]] already proves it, and the companion page's
   110	[[ex-ivt-gives-nth-roots]] records the second proof without minting a second
   111	name for the same statement.
   112	
   113	**A converse worth having.**
   114	[[lem-monotone-with-interval-image-is-continuous]] proves that a function on an
   115	interval satisfying $f(x) \le f(y)$ whenever $x \le y$, whose image is
   116	order-convex, is continuous. It needs no one-sided limits of monotone functions,
   117	which are not available at this point in the reading order; the whole proof
   118	reads the required $\delta$ off the image. It is stated as a standalone lemma so
   119	that a later page may cite it — the classical application is a monotone function
   120	built by a limiting construction whose image is known independently.
   121	
   122	**Uniform continuity, and the dictionary.**
   123	[[def-uniform-continuity-real]] asks for one $\delta$ serving every pair of
   124	points of $A$; the whole content is that $\exists \delta$ moves to the left of
   125	the point quantifier. [[lem-real-and-metric-notions-agree]] is then the hinge of
   126	the page. It proves that, for $A \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ with $d(x,y) = |x-y|$,
   127	continuity and uniform continuity in the senses defined here coincide with
   128	[[def-metric-continuity]] and [[def-metric-uniform-continuity]]; that the
   129	Lipschitz and Hölder conditions for a real function *are*
   130	[[def-lipschitz-holder-contraction]] instantiated, and are not redefined here;
   131	and — the clause that closes a seam nobody had flagged — that a subset of
   132	$\mathbb{R}$ is compact in the open-cover sense of [[def-open-cover-r]] exactly
   133	when it is a compact metric subspace, with the same agreement for sequential
   134	compactness. Two consequences are recorded: the implications of
   135	[[thm-metric-regularity-hierarchy]] transfer verbatim to real functions, and
   136	Cauchy sequences of $A$ are the same objects in both vocabularies.
   137	
   138	**Why three theorems are stated twice.** The continuous image of a compact set,
   139	the extreme value theorem and Heine-Cantor appear on this page in
   140	$\mathbb{R}$-native form and on the metric-spaces page in metric-general form,
   141	with distinct identifiers and distinct proofs. That is deliberate. The proofs
   142	here run through [[thm-heine-borel-characterisation-r]] and
   143	[[thm-compact-iff-sequentially-compact-r]], which are order-based; the metric
   144	proofs run through the cover machinery of metric spaces.
   145	[[lem-real-and-metric-notions-agree]] is the single place in the library where
   146	the duplication is acknowledged, and each duplicated item links it.
   147	
   148	**Heine-Cantor, extension, and the two sharp statements.**
   149	[[thm-heine-cantor-r]] proves that a continuous function on a compact
   150	$K \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ is uniformly continuous, by contradiction from
   151	sequential compactness, with countable choice used exactly once and named.
   152	[[cor-continuous-extension-from-a-dense-subset-r]] proves that a uniformly
   153	continuous function on a nonempty $D \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ extends uniquely to a
   154	uniformly continuous function on $\overline{D}$; that is the statement later
   155	pages need in order to define a function on the rationals first and pass to the
   156	reals. [[thm-holder-exponent-above-one-forces-constancy]] explains why
   157	[[def-lipschitz-holder-contraction]] stops at exponent $1$: on an interval, an
   158	inequality $|f(x) - f(y)| \le C|x-y|^{\alpha}$ with rational $\alpha > 1$ forces
   159	$f$ to be constant. Finally
   160	[[thm-compactness-is-necessary-for-evt-and-uniform-continuity]] is the sharp
   161	converse to everything above: on a set that is not compact there is always an
   162	unbounded continuous function and a bounded continuous function whose supremum
   163	is not attained, and if that set is bounded there is also a continuous function
   164	that is not uniformly continuous. All four witness functions are exhibited
   165	explicitly.
   166	
   167	**The three false statements** on this page each carry their own witness,
   168	verified in full: that continuity implies uniform continuity
   169	([[fs-continuity-implies-uniform-continuity]], refuted by $1/x$ on $(0,1)$),
   170	that a continuous image of a closed set is closed
   171	([[fs-continuous-image-of-a-closed-set-is-closed]], refuted by
   172	$1/(1+x^{2})$ on $\mathbb{R}$, whose image is $(0,1]$), and that boundedness of
   173	the domain suffices for the extreme value theorem
   174	([[fs-evt-holds-on-every-bounded-domain]], refuted by the identity on $(0,1)$).
   175	The companion page works the first and the third out again in more detail and
   176	adds the witnesses for the regularity hierarchy, the Dirichlet function, and the
   177	two examples that turn the intermediate value theorem into a computation.
