items/thm-heine-cantor-r.md-50-statements are the same statement in two vocabularies is
items/thm-heine-cantor-r.md-51-[[lem-real-and-metric-notions-agree]], clauses 1, 2 and 5, immediately above.
items/thm-heine-cantor-r.md-52-
items/thm-heine-cantor-r.md:53:**The choice cost, named.** The proof invokes the axiom of countable choice
items/thm-heine-cantor-r.md-54-([[def-countable-choice]]) exactly once, at step 3.1, to select one bad pair of
items/thm-heine-cantor-r.md-55-points from each of countably many nonempty sets. The backward implication of
items/thm-heine-cantor-r.md:56:[[thm-compact-iff-sequentially-compact-r]] also spends countable choice, and
items/thm-heine-cantor-r.md-57-that item names its own uses; the forward implication used here, from compact to
items/thm-heine-cantor-r.md-58-sequentially compact, does not. No claim is made that the axiom is necessary
items/thm-heine-cantor-r.md-59-for either.
